Final Model
The workflow-set comparison above told us the random forest wins. Let’s fit it once more on the full training set so we have a single model object to interrogate:

final &lt;- workflow() |&gt;
  add_recipe(rec) |&gt;
  add_model(rf_model) |&gt;
  fit(data = camels_train)

Recipe (30) — Week 5-1

Define a formula you want to use to predict logQmean
In 5–7 sentences, justify your predictor choice. Consult the downloaded PDF; ground each recipe step (log, normalize, interact, naomit, impute, …) in what the EDA above showed.
Build a recipe that handles those predictors. Your recipe should contain at least three step_* calls beyond the formula, and each should be there for a reason you can name.


All numeric predictor variables were included except those derived from streamflow, which would cause issues since they are computed from the outcome variable. Geographic coordinates were also excluded as they represent spatial location rather than physical process. Exploratory data analysis revealed that several predictors are heavily right-skewed, justifying the use of step_log() on those variables. Variables frac_snow and max_water_content are bounded between 0 and 1 and were excluded from log transformation. step_impute_mean() was applied to handle the small number of missing values in root_depth_50, root_depth_99, and geol_porostiy. step_normalize() was applied to all predictors to place them on a common scale, which is important for ensuring no single variable dominates the model due to differences in units or magnitude. Finally, step_naomit was used as a safety to catch any remaining rows with missing values.


recipe &lt;- recipe(logQmean ~ ., data = camels_train) |&gt;
  step_rm(q_mean, runoff_ratio, slope_fdc, baseflow_index, stream_elas,
          q5, q95, high_q_freq, high_q_dur, low_q_freq, low_q_dur,
          zero_q_freq, hfd_mean, gauge_lat, gauge_lon, gauge_id, high_prec_timing, low_prec_timing,           geol_1st_class, geol_2nd_class, dom_land_cover) |&gt;
  step_impute_mean(root_depth_50, root_depth_99, geol_porostiy) |&gt;
  step_log(aridity, elev_mean, p_mean, slope_mean, soil_conductivity) |&gt;
  step_normalize(all_predictors()) |&gt;
  step_naomit(all_predictors(), all_outcomes())

Define 3 models (30) — Week 6-1

Define a random forest model using the rand_forest function (engine ranger, mode regression)
Define two other models of your choice. The Week 6-1 zoo (linear_reg, boost_tree, svm_rbf, svm_poly, mlp, bag_mlp, decision_tree) is a good menu — the comparison table in that deck summarizes strengths and weaknesses.
For each model, briefly note (one sentence) why it’s a reasonable candidate for this regression problem — what kind of structure does it capture?


rf_model &lt;- rand_forest() |&gt;
  set_engine("ranger", importance = "impurity") |&gt;
  set_mode("regression")


A random forest model is a bagging tree ensemble learning method that handles non-linearity well and is typically robust as is, requiring little tuning.


xgboost_model &lt;- boost_tree() |&gt;
  set_engine("xgboost") |&gt;
  set_mode("regression")


A boost tree model a boosting tree ensemble learning method and is often super accurate for tabular data and can capture complex interactions.


dt_model &lt;- decision_tree() |&gt;
  set_engine("rpart") |&gt;
  set_mode("regression")


A decision tree model is a visual model that handles non-linearity well and can provide a good baseline to compare the other ensemble models against.

Variable importance (25) — Week 5-2

Produce a vip::vip() plot for your tuned model (pattern: extract_fit_parsnip() |&gt; vip())
Interpret the top predictors (4–6 sentences). Cross-reference against the predictor Moran’s I you computed in EDA — are any of the top VIP features also highly spatially autocorrelated? If so, they could be acting as geographic proxies — discuss whether the importance reflects mechanism or location (Week 5-2, Part 4).
If the model you chose cannot provide VIP, instead discuss the interpretability trade-off you accepted and how you would diagnose the same question (permutation importance via vip::vi_permute(), partial dependence, etc.).


best_params &lt;- select_best(rf_tune, metric = "rsq")

rf_final &lt;- workflow() |&gt;
  add_recipe(recipe) |&gt;
  add_model(rf_model_tuned) |&gt;
  finalize_workflow(best_params) |&gt;
  fit(data = camels_train)

rf_final |&gt;
  extract_fit_parsnip() |&gt;
  vip()









The top predictors by a large margin are p_mean and aridity. Both are climate drivers of streamflow and reflect a strong physical mechanism. However, they also have a comparatively high Moran’s I value, with p_mean = .430 and aridity = .352. This could mean their importance may partly reflect location rather than physical procesesses alone. However, the highest Moran’s I value belonged to slope_mean at .440, which had the least variable importance. This signifies that location does not necessarily translate into predictive power in this model.
